# [Applications and serving APIs]($heading.id('serving-apis'))

[Applications and agents]($text.id('component-applications')) send HTTP requests to
[LLMD]($text.id('component-llmd')). The serving engine runs the model and the endpoint determines what the client receives.

| Endpoint | Result |
| --- | --- |
| [Chat completions]($link.page('llmd/api-reference/chat-completions')) · [/v1/chat/completions]($text.id('component-chat-completions')) | An OpenAI-compatible client or SDK sends messages. LLMD returns a completion or streams the generated text. |
| [Embeddings]($link.page('llmd/api-reference/embeddings')) · [/v1/embeddings]($text.id('component-embeddings')) | The model pools its output on the device and returns a vector for each input text. |
| [Decision]($link.page('llmd/api-reference/decision')) · [/v1/systemone]($text.id('component-decision')) | The client supplies one state and a set of yes/no, choice or score questions. A question can depend on an earlier answer. |

For [observability]($text.id('component-observability')), LLMD exposes Prometheus metrics and provides crash reports.
See [Monitor and troubleshoot LLMD]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/operations/monitoring-and-troubleshooting').ref('scrape-service-metrics')) to inspect service metrics during a run.

# [Serving engine]($heading.id('engine'))

The [engine]($text.id('component-engine')) prepares input, schedules model execution and returns results to clients.

- **[Chat templates.]($text.id('component-chat-templates'))** For chat, a Jinja2 engine in Zig formats messages into the prompt expected by the model.
- **[Tokenizers.]($text.id('component-tokenizers'))** The tokenizer converts that prompt into token IDs. The map lists BPE, WordPiece and SentencePiece.
- **[Output parsers.]($text.id('component-output-parsers'))** On the return path, parsers extract reasoning traces and tool calls from generated content.
- **[Scheduler.]($text.id('component-scheduler'))** Continuous batching mixes prefill and decode work from active requests. Long prefills can be split into chunks.
- **[Paged KV cache.]($text.id('component-paged-kv-cache'))** Pages hold attention keys and values. Requests with a shared prefix can reuse cached state.
- **[Speculative decoding.]($text.id('component-speculative-decoding'))** A drafter proposes tokens for the target model to verify. The map names DSpark, DFlash and DSpine. Find [external drafters for your model]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/model-compatibility').unsafeRef('external-drafters')).
- **[Async execution.]($text.id('component-async-execution'))** The host prepares the next step while the device runs the current one.
- **[Parallelism.]($text.id('component-parallelism'))** Tensor and expert parallelism distribute parts of a model across devices. Data parallelism distributes inputs across replicas.
- **[Weight preparation.]($text.id('component-weight-preparation'))** The engine can fuse, stack or swizzle weights into the layout a kernel expects. The map gives FP8, MXFP4 and NVFP4 as examples.

See [Batching and memory]($link.page('llmd/cli-reference/batching-and-memory')) for the flags that control scheduling and cache capacity.

# [DeepSeek V4.1-Flash example]($heading.id('deepseek-v4-1-flash'))

[DeepSeek V4.1-Flash]($text.id('component-model')) is the Zig model expanded in the map.

1. **[Embedding.]($text.id('component-embedding'))** Look up a vector for each input token ID.
2. **[Decoder layer × N.]($text.id('component-decoder-layer'))** Pass the vectors through the repeated decoder layers. The table below expands one layer.
3. **[LM head.]($text.id('component-lm-head'))** Project the decoder output to scores over the token vocabulary.
4. **[Sampling.]($text.id('component-sampling'))** Select the next token using those scores.

The optional [DSpark drafter]($text.id('component-dspark-drafter')) uses one decoder layer to propose tokens for the target model to verify.

| Inside a decoder layer | Role |
| --- | --- |
| [Engram]($text.id('component-engram')) | On selected layers, the host computes n-gram hashes for lookups in host-pinned tables. A gate controls how much of the result is added to the residual stream. |
| [Sparse MLA attention]($text.id('component-sparse-mla-attention')) | Latent query and KV projections use YaRN rotary embeddings. A KV compressor and top-k indexer support sliding-window and sparse attention with attention sinks, followed by a grouped output projection. |
| [Mixture of experts]($text.id('component-mixture-of-experts')) | A router selects the top-k MXFP4 routed experts. The layer also has a shared expert and can distribute experts across devices. |
| [mHC]($text.id('component-mhc')) | Mixes residual streams before and after attention and expert computation. |
| [Paged caches]($text.id('component-paged-caches')) | Window KV uses FP8. Compressed KV and indexer keys use FP4. |

The [model compatibility catalog]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/model-compatibility')) lists the published launch recipes for this model.

# [Kernel implementations]($heading.id('kernels'))

The [kernel matrix]($text.id('component-kernels')) maps five operation families to eight platforms.
A cell names a platform-specific implementation or portable StableHLO. A dash means the diagram leaves that implementation unspecified.

- **[Attention.]($text.id('component-kernel-attention'))** Includes MLA and grouped-query attention.
- **[Indexer.]($text.id('component-kernel-indexer'))** Selects candidates for sparse attention. DSA and QSA are examples.
- **[Gated DeltaNet.]($text.id('component-kernel-deltanet'))** Six platforms list custom kernels. The map leaves the Trainium and CPU entries unspecified.
- **[Mixture of experts.]($text.id('component-kernel-experts'))** Routed expert computation uses a custom kernel on NVIDIA, AMD, Google TPU, Apple and Furiosa. The other three targets use StableHLO in the map.
- **[mHC.]($text.id('component-kernel-mhc'))** Residual-stream mixing uses CuTe on NVIDIA, Fly on AMD and StableHLO on the other six targets.

| Platform | Attention | Indexer | Gated DeltaNet | Mixture of experts | mHC |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| NVIDIA | CuTe · Triton | Triton | CuTe · Triton | CuTe · Triton | CuTe |
| AMD | Fly · Triton | Triton | Fly · Triton | Fly · Triton | Fly |
| Google TPU | Mosaic | StableHLO | Mosaic | Mosaic | StableHLO |
| Trainium | NKI | StableHLO | — | StableHLO | StableHLO |
| Intel | Triton | StableHLO | Triton | StableHLO | StableHLO |
| Apple | Metal | StableHLO | Metal | Metal | StableHLO |
| Furiosa | TCL | StableHLO | TCL | TCL | StableHLO |
| CPU | StableHLO | StableHLO | — | StableHLO | StableHLO |

# [ZML compilation and execution]($heading.id('zml'))

[ZML]($text.id('component-zml')) compiles model code into an executable and loads weights into device buffers for that executable.

- **[Tensor API.]($text.id('component-tensor-api'))** Zig model code expresses matrix multiplication, gather, scan and sort. For example, `q.dot(k, .hd).softmax(.k)` computes a dot product followed by softmax. See the [Tensor API]($link.page('zml/api-reference/tensor')).
- **[Compiler.]($text.id('component-compiler'))** ZML generates MLIR using dialects including `stablehlo`, `sdy` and `func`, with one program per shape bucket.
- **[Kernel languages.]($text.id('component-kernel-languages'))** The compiler can embed custom kernels in the graph. The diagram names Triton, CuTe, CUDA Tile, FlyDSL, Mosaic, NKI, Metal and TCL.
- **[Sharding.]($text.id('component-sharding'))** Tensor shardings describe placement on a device mesh with named axes. XLA partitions the computation accordingly. See [Run ZML across devices]($link.page('zml/developer-guide/run-across-devices')).
- **[I/O.]($text.id('component-io'))** ZML reads tensors from Hugging Face, S3, Google Cloud Storage, HTTP or disk and moves data between host and device using DMA. See [Load and inspect model weights]($link.page('zml/developer-guide/load-and-inspect-model-weights')).
- **[Execution.]($text.id('component-execution'))** The runtime launches a compiled executable asynchronously with device buffers holding parameters and inputs. See [Exe]($link.page('zml/api-reference/exe')).
- **[PJRT C API.]($text.id('component-pjrt'))** ZML uses a common plugin interface for compilation, data transfer and execution on each platform. See the [OpenXLA source](https://github.com/openxla/xla).

# [Platforms]($heading.id('platforms'))

The [framework targets]($text.id('component-platforms')) below show each platform’s runtime and kernel languages.
To deploy a server, check the [LLMD distributions]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/reference/hardware-compatibility'))
and the [model recipes]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/model-compatibility')) for your hardware.

| Framework target | Runtime or architecture | Kernel languages shown |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [NVIDIA]($text.id('component-nvidia')) | CUDA | CuTe, CUDA Tile, Triton |
| [AMD]($text.id('component-amd')) | ROCm | FlyDSL, Triton |
| [Google TPU]($text.id('component-google-tpu')) | libtpu | Mosaic |
| [AWS Trainium]($text.id('component-trainium')) | Neuron | NKI |
| [Intel]($text.id('component-intel')) | oneAPI | Triton |
| [Apple]($text.id('component-apple')) | Metal | Metal |
| [Furiosa]($text.id('component-furiosa')) | Furiosa SDK | TCL |
| [CPU]($text.id('component-cpu')) | x86, Arm | — |
